WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.594
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:09.280
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:17.910
Unit two: Scientific Discourse. Today you will describe obligation, possibility and procedure in science with modal passives.

4
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.944
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.994
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:26.990
Empirical.

7
00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:32.247
Empirical means based on observation or experiment rather than theory alone.

8
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.861
The claim lacks empirical support.

9
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.835
Your turn. Say: Empirical.

10
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:41.759
Empirical.

11
00:00:42.517 --> 00:00:43.518
Replicate.

12
00:00:44.517 --> 00:00:47.875
Replicate means to repeat a study to see if the results hold.

13
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:50.553
The findings could not be replicated.

14
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.547
Your turn. Say: Replicate.

15
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.529
Replicate.

16
00:00:58.283 --> 00:00:59.238
Peer-reviewed.

17
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.968
Peer-reviewed means checked by independent experts before publication.

18
00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:07.152
Cite peer-reviewed journals, not blogs.

19
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.518
Your turn. Say: Peer-reviewed.

20
00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:14.286
Peer-reviewed.

21
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:15.900
Theory.

22
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:20.402
Theory means a well-tested explanation of natural phenomena.

23
00:01:21.017 --> 00:01:23.875
In science, a theory is far more than a guess.

24
00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:26.741
Your turn. Say: Theory.

25
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.264
Theory.

26
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:32.354
Falsifiable.

27
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.952
Falsifiable means capable of being proven wrong by evidence.

28
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.246
A scientific claim must be falsifiable.

29
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.013
Your turn. Say: Falsifiable.

30
00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:48.135
Falsifiable.

31
00:01:48.883 --> 00:01:49.664
Rigor.

32
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.532
Rigor means strict care and precision in method.

33
00:01:54.117 --> 00:01:56.256
The study was praised for its rigor.

34
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.073
Your turn. Say: Rigor.

35
00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:02.714
Rigor.

36
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.741
Consensus.

37
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:09.340
Consensus means general agreement among experts.

38
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.635
There is a strong consensus on climate change.

39
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.251
Your turn. Say: Consensus.

40
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.275
Consensus.

41
00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:22.064
Innovation.

42
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.385
Innovation means a new method, idea or product.

43
00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:29.900
Innovation depends on reliable basic research.

44
00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:32.774
Your turn. Say: Innovation.

45
00:02:35.633 --> 00:02:36.658
Innovation.

46
00:02:37.417 --> 00:02:38.406
Anomaly.

47
00:02:39.417 --> 00:02:42.891
Anomaly means a result that does not fit the expected pattern.

48
00:02:43.483 --> 00:02:46.087
One anomaly led to a major discovery.

49
00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:49.022
Your turn. Say: Anomaly.

50
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:52.810
Anomaly.

51
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:54.574
Paradigm.

52
00:02:55.583 --> 00:02:58.930
Paradigm means a dominant framework of assumptions in a field.

53
00:02:59.517 --> 00:03:02.306
Einstein's work caused a paradigm shift.

54
00:03:02.917 --> 00:03:05.639
Your turn. Say: Paradigm.

55
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:09.408
Paradigm.

56
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.373
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:21.015
Visal, your lab report says the results 'prove' the hypothesis. Would a scientist phrase it that way?

58
00:03:22.117 --> 00:03:30.470
Probably not. Results can only support a hypothesis; they should be replicated before anyone treats them as reliable.

59
00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:34.407
And what about the one sample that behaved strangely?

60
00:03:35.517 --> 00:03:44.229
That anomaly must be reported, not deleted. It might be explained by contamination, or it could point to something new.

61
00:03:45.317 --> 00:03:47.200
Why does that matter so much?

62
00:03:48.317 --> 00:03:59.944
Because a theory is only scientific if it is falsifiable. Inconvenient data has to be taken seriously, or the whole method loses its rigor.

63
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.565
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:03.417 --> 00:04:05.927
Grammar focus: Passive with modals.

65
00:04:06.227 --> 00:04:07.484
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:08.183 --> 00:04:09.858
Theories must be tested.

67
00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.597
The results should be replicated by an independent team.

68
00:04:14.483 --> 00:04:17.423
The anomaly might have been caused by contamination.

69
00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:28.368
Modal passives (must be done, could be explained, should have been checked) keep the focus on the process, not the scientist. Use 'modal + have been + past participle' for the past.

70
00:04:29.117 --> 00:04:32.709
Be careful! Don't say: The results must be replicate.

71
00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:37.537
Say: The results must be replicated.

72
00:04:37.837 --> 00:04:41.253
Modal passive: modal + be + past participle.

73
00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:44.568
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:47.343
How Science Earns Trust.

75
00:04:47.643 --> 00:04:52.591
Scientific knowledge is provisional: every claim can be challenged by new evidence.

76
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.036
Before a finding is accepted, it should be replicated by researchers working independently.

77
00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:06.770
Anomalies must not be ignored, since a single unexpected result can expose a weakness in the dominant paradigm.

78
00:05:07.767 --> 00:05:12.169
Peer review, though imperfect, filters out many errors before publication.

79
00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:19.427
Consensus, therefore, is not the opinion of a majority but the product of evidence that has survived repeated testing.

80
00:05:20.533 --> 00:05:24.605
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.042
The data blank checked before it was published.

82
00:05:28.342 --> 00:05:32.587
A, should have been. B, should have. C, should be had.

83
00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:38.319
The answer is A: should have been.

84
00:05:39.483 --> 00:05:42.644
A claim that no possible evidence could disprove is not blank.

85
00:05:42.944 --> 00:05:47.294
A, empirical. B, falsifiable. C, replicated.

86
00:05:50.833 --> 00:05:53.007
The answer is B: falsifiable.

87
00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.103
Which collocation is correct?

88
00:05:56.403 --> 00:06:01.392
A, make a consensus. B, do a consensus. C, reach a consensus.

89
00:06:04.933 --> 00:06:07.804
The answer is C: reach a consensus.

90
00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:11.205
Choose the best academic phrasing.

91
00:06:11.505 --> 00:06:19.442
A, The results prove we were right.. B, The results appear to support the hypothesis.. C, The results totally show it works..

92
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.938
The answer is B: The results appear to support the hypothesis..

93
00:06:28.083 --> 00:06:31.197
According to the reading, scientific consensus is…

94
00:06:31.497 --> 00:06:39.389
A, evidence that has survived repeated testing. B, the opinion of most scientists. C, a finding published once.

95
00:06:42.933 --> 00:06:46.593
The answer is A: evidence that has survived repeated testing.

96
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:51.184
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.746
Why must scientific theories be falsifiable?

98
00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:13.166
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:46.545
I believe a theory must be falsifiable because science advances by testing ideas against evidence. If no possible result could prove a claim wrong, then the claim cannot really be tested at all. For instance, astrology can explain any outcome after the event, so it can never be checked. Some argue that this standard is too strict for fields like economics. Nevertheless, falsifiability remains the clearest line between science and belief.

100
00:07:46.845 --> 00:07:49.181
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:07:50.083 --> 00:07:54.187
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and passive with modals.

102
00:07:54.487 --> 00:07:59.322
Today's value is rigor. Check every step carefully before you trust a conclusion.

103
00:07:59.622 --> 00:08:02.193
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

104
00:08:02.493 --> 00:08:05.668
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

